 ## Footprints and parallel contention
 
-Contracts are only allowed to access ledger entries specified in the footprint of their transaction. Transactions with overlapping footprints are said to contend, and will only execute sequentially with respect to one another, on a single thread. Transactions with non-overlapping footprints may execute in parallel. This means that a finer granularity of `CONTRACT_DATA` ledger entries may reduce artificial contention among transactions using a contract, and thereby increase parallelism.
+Contracts are only allowed to access ledger entries specified in the footprint of their transaction. Transactions conflict when they share a ledger key and at least one transaction lists that key as read-write. A shared read-only key does not create this conflict. Conflicting transactions execute sequentially relative to one another. Nonconflicting transactions may execute in parallel, subject to transaction-set scheduling and network limits. This means that a finer granularity of `CONTRACT_DATA` ledger entries may reduce artificial contention among transactions using a contract, and thereby increase parallelism.
 
